// pescOptionSetDerivedEval.recipe.jsonc — LUNAR_PRISM, 2026-08-17. Created under RULING P2-R5.
//
// THE SIBLING OF pescDerivedEval. PESC and the CEDS hub, plus the PESC -> CEDS derived bridge over the
// OPTION-SET TIER — pescOptionSetCedsDerivedPlugin over `PescNamedDefinition`, 223 concepts.
//
// ============================ WHY THIS FILE EXISTS AT ALL ============================
// NOT because the option-set tier is a lesser thing, and NOT because the two-plugin design was wrong.
// Both plugins are shipped, validated, and censused. They cannot share a MANIFEST, and that is a
// property of the manifest subject composer rather than of either declaration.
//
// [code fact] vocabulary.js:183-198 composes a relationship subject from exactly five inputs —
// hubStandard, hubVersion, sourceStandard, sourceVersion, producer. THE PLUGIN IS NOT AMONG THEM.
// [code fact] build.js:1740-1745 derives producer 'inferred' for any block carrying a decisionBlock,
// and both PESC plugins are frozen-judge derived plugins. Both therefore compose the identical subject
// and manifestEditor refuses the second by name. The full account is in pescDerivedEval's header.
//
// WHAT THIS SPLIT COSTS, STATED RATHER THAN GLOSSED. Running both plugins in one build was not a
// convenience — it put both populations' pool statistics in ONE run, so they could be reported
// separately with no possibility of a stale comparison between runs. That guarantee is gone. THE
// POPULATIONS STILL MUST NEVER BE POOLED, and now any report citing both tiers MUST NAME THE RUN each
// figure came from, because two runs is exactly the condition under which a stale figure can be quoted
// beside a fresh one and look like a comparison.
//
// ============================ WHAT DIFFERS FROM THE SIBLING ============================
// Only the bridge line. The standards, the hub, and the round-trip stage are identical BY DESIGN — this
// is the same PESC/CEDS pairing seen through a second SUBJECT SHAPE, which is the whole point the two
// plugins were built to demonstrate: the seam admits a second subject shape with ZERO framework change.
//
//   subjectSource label    PescNamedDefinition (223 option-set concepts), not PescElementDecl (2,213)
//   predicateByCategory    relatedMatch on ALL THREE judge categories — a RECORDED UNDERSTATEMENT
//                          rather than a measured judgement. See bridgeData/pescOptionSetUnderstatementRecord.json.
//   dedup key              (family, kind, name, description) -> 223. The narrower (family, kind, name)
//                          key of PLAN §1-A yields 156 and is UNSOUND: 67 of its 156 groups (43%) differ
//                          internally in the RENDERED `description`. Superseded by RULING P1-R13, not
//                          overlooked. Cross-check: (name, kind, description) gives 222, differing by
//                          exactly one concept that appears in two families.
//
// ROUND-TRIP STAGE ON, for the same reason as the sibling: a bridge writes edges, never nodes, so the
// PESC round trip must remain numerically identical to its pin with the bridge present.
//
// SPEND: the DEBUG judge costs nothing. A REAL-judge line is authorised PER LINE, as data, in
// acceptanceCommands.jsonc — never by editing this file.
{
	"schemaVersion": "1.0.0",
	"recipeName": "pescOptionSetDerivedEval",
	"kind": "dev",
	"description": "PESC and the CEDS hub only, PLUS the PESC -> CEDS DERIVED bridge over the OPTION-SET TIER (pescOptionSetCedsDerivedPlugin over PescNamedDefinition, 223 concepts). The sibling of pescDerivedEval, split from it under RULING P2-R5 because two derived plugins over one standard pair compose an identical manifest subject and cannot share a manifest.",
	"standards": [
		{ "token": "ceds", "version": "current" },
		{ "token": "pesc260805", "version": "current" }
	],
	"hubs": [
		{ "standard": "ceds" }
	],
	"bridges": [
		{ "source": "pesc260805", "hub": "ceds", "bridge": "pescOptionSetCedsDerivedPlugin", "dependencies": ["pesc260805", "ceds"] }
	],
	"roundTripStage": true
}
